---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RoiCalculator from '@/components/tools/finance/RoiCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="roi-calculator">
  <RoiCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the ROI calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Enter the <strong>amount invested</strong> and any <strong>additional costs</strong>: commission,
        stamp duty, legal fees, renovation, ad spend. Costs belong in the denominator, or the ROI is too high.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter the <strong>final value</strong> and any <strong>income received</strong> during the period,
        such as dividends, rent or interest.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set the <strong>holding period</strong>, as a duration or as the actual buy and sell dates.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set a <strong>benchmark return</strong>: what the same money would probably have earned elsewhere. An
        investment is good if it beats the benchmark; a profit alone does not show that.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>ROI and annualised ROI</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>ROI</strong> is total gain over total cost:
      <code>ROI = (final value + income − invested − costs) / (invested + costs)</code>. It ignores how long
      you held the investment.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Annualised ROI</strong>, the compound annual growth rate, is the constant yearly rate that gives
      the same result: <code>CAGR = (end / start)^(1/t) − 1</code>. It is the only figure you can compare
      across investments of different lengths.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Total ROI</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">over 1 year</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">2 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">3 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">5 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">10 years</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>10%</td><td style="text-align:right">10.00%</td><td style="text-align:right">4.88%</td><td style="text-align:right">3.23%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.92%</td><td style="text-align:right">0.96%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>25%</td><td style="text-align:right">25.00%</td><td style="text-align:right">11.80%</td><td style="text-align:right">7.72%</td><td style="text-align:right">4.56%</td><td style="text-align:right">2.26%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50%</td><td style="text-align:right">50.00%</td><td style="text-align:right">22.47%</td><td style="text-align:right">14.47%</td><td style="text-align:right">8.45%</td><td style="text-align:right">4.14%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100%</td><td style="text-align:right">100.00%</td><td style="text-align:right">41.42%</td><td style="text-align:right">25.99%</td><td style="text-align:right">14.87%</td><td style="text-align:right">7.18%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>200%</td><td style="text-align:right">200.00%</td><td style="text-align:right">73.21%</td><td style="text-align:right">44.22%</td><td style="text-align:right">24.57%</td><td style="text-align:right">11.61%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A "100% return" over one year is excellent. Over ten years it is 7.18% a year, below the long-run
      average of a broad equity index. For this reason, headline ROI figures in marketing material seldom
      give the holding period.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting the inputs right</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Count every cost: purchase commission, exchange fees, spread, legal costs, survey fees, renovation,
        storage, insurance. For a marketing campaign, count agency fees and staff time as well as media spend.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter income separately from the final value. Rent and dividends are real returns, even though they
        never show as a price rise.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use the net sale proceeds. Selling costs come out of the final value, not the starting one.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter the real holding period. Date mode uses a 365.2425-day year, so leap years do not distort short
        holds.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When ROI is the wrong measure</h2>
    <p>
      ROI assumes one amount in at the start and one amount out at the end. If you added or withdrew money
      during the period, such as monthly contributions to a fund, staged payments on a property development
      or a second ad budget halfway through, the timing of those flows changes the true return. Only an
      internal rate of return (IRR) or money-weighted return handles that correctly. ROI overstates the
      result when money went in late and understates it when money came out early.
    </p>
    <p>
      ROI also ignores risk. A 12% annualised return from a diversified index and a 12% annualised return
      from a single speculative holding are different results, and the arithmetic does not show it.
    </p>

    <h2>Inflation and tax</h2>
    <p>
      The figures here are nominal and before tax. For the real return, divide by cumulative inflation over
      the same period; the inflation calculator gives the factor. For the after-tax return, apply your
      capital gains and dividend or rental income tax rates to the relevant parts of the profit before you
      compute ROI.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
